; ============================================================
; TITLE:    The Ackermann Function
; CATEGORY: Recursion
; LEVEL:    Advanced
; TAGS:     recursion, nested recursion, growth, call stack depth
; SUMMARY:  Explores a famous function whose recursion feeds its own answer back into itself, counting calls and measuring how deep the stack goes.
; ============================================================
;
; WHAT IT DOES
;   The Ackermann function takes two numbers m and n:
;     A(0, n) = n + 1
;     A(m, 0) = A(m - 1, 1)
;     A(m, n) = A(m - 1, A(m, n - 1))
;   The last rule is the strange one: the inner call's answer becomes
;   an INPUT of the outer call. The answers stay small for a while and
;   then grow faster than any power. This program prints a table for
;   small inputs, how many calls each one took and how deep the call
;   stack got. Only tiny inputs are safe: A(4, 1) already needs
;   nearly three billion calls.
;
; HOW TO USE
;   1. Press Run and look at the table.
;   2. Put a breakpoint on the line
;        output ack :m - 1 ack :m :n - 1
;      and choose "Stop only when..." with  :depth > 6  in the right-click
;      menu of the red dot. Run. Open the Call Stack and click down
;      through it: m and n jump around in a way that is hard to predict.
;   3. Add watches for  :calls  and  :deepest  and press F5 a few times.
;
; CONCEPTS YOU WILL LEARN
;   * nested recursion, where a recursive call is the input to another
;   * using global counters to measure what a procedure does
;   * why some correct programs are still impossible to run
;
; THINGS TO TRY
;   * Find a formula for A(1, n) and A(2, n) from the table.
;   * Try ack 3 6. Try ack 3 7 if you are patient.
;   * Do NOT try ack 4 1 unless you want to press Stop.
; ============================================================

make "calls 0
make "depth 0
make "deepest 0

; :depth counts how many ACK calls are unfinished right now. It goes
; up on the way in and down on the way out, and :deepest remembers the
; highest it has been.
to ack :m :n
  make "calls :calls + 1
  make "depth :depth + 1
  if :depth > :deepest [make "deepest :depth]
  localmake "answer ack.rule :m :n
  make "depth :depth - 1
  output :answer
end

; The three rules, one per line, exactly as written in the comment above.
to ack.rule :m :n
  if :m = 0 [output :n + 1]
  if :n = 0 [output ack :m - 1 1]
  output ack :m - 1 ack :m :n - 1
end

; Prints the answer in a fixed-width column so the table lines up.
to try :m :n
  make "calls 0
  make "deepest 0
  localmake "a ack :m :n
  print (sentence (word "|A(| :m ", :n "|)|) "= (form :a 5 0) "| | ~
    (form :calls 8 0) [calls, stack depth] :deepest)
end

print [m and n, the answer, how many calls, how deep]
for [m 0 3] [
  for [n 0 4] [try :m :n]
  print []
]
